\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 12 & 5\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ 12 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534816 17592186045417 true] [17592186436956 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436962 13194139534816 17592186045417 true] [17592186436956 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436963 13194139534816 17592186045417 true] [17592186436956 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436964 13194139534816 17592186045417 true] [17592186436956 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436965 13194139534816 17592186045417 true] [17592186436956 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436966 13194139534816 17592186045417 true] [17592186436956 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534816 17592186045417 true] [17592186436956 1045 "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix" 13194139534816 17592186045417 true] [